  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows general onshore moving \nscattered showers and lightning storms across east central Florida. \nGOES-16 satellite imagery and local observations show partly to \nmostly cloudy skies over east central Florida. Local temperatures \nare currently in the mid 80s to low 90s with dew points in the mid \n70s to around 80F degrees. The 10Z XMR sounding shows plenty of\nmoisture in place with a PWAT of 2.20\", in addition to MidRH at \n85% and LowRH at 83%. However, instability and bulk shear are\nrelatively weak. Analysis charts show a stationary boundary \nacross central Florida to the south of Cape Canaveral. \n\nScattered to numerous showers (~40-70% chance) and lightning \nstorms are forecast to increase in coverage into this evening with\ncollisions between onshore flow, in addition to the west coast \nsea breeze over the western interior, as well as outflow from \nprevious storms. Rain chances are forecast to gradually diminish \naround 9PM. However, isolated to scattered onshore moving showers \nand lightning storms are forecast near the coast through the early\nmorning on Wednesday. The potential exists for isolated strong \nstorms capable of occasional to frequent lightning, wind gusts up\nto 40-50mph, and heavy rainfall (up to 1-3\" in 60 minutes). For\nthis reason a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall exists for much\nof east central Florida today mainly along and south of the\nOrlando metro. Above normal lows (~1F-3F+) in the low to upper \n70s are expected. \n\nWednesday-Thursday... An active week of weather is expected with  \nonshore flow in place coupled with a weak stationary boundary over\neast central Florida or to the north through Thursday. Scattered\nto numerous showers/lightning storms are forecast to develop each\nafternoon and evening. The greatest coverage of the shower and \nlightning storm activity is expected between 12pm-8pm. East- \nsoutheast winds at 5-10mph are forecast each afternoon and \nevening. The potential exists for isolated strong storms capable \nof frequent lightning, wind gusts up to 40-50mph, and heavy \nrainfall (up to 1-3\" in 60 minutes). Afternoon highs in the upper\n80s to low 90s and lows in the low to upper 70s are forecast. A\nModerate HeatRisk with small pockets of Major HeatRisk across\nnorthern east central Florida is forecast each day. \n\nFriday-Monday... A return to a more typical sea breeze pattern is\nexpected with the Bermuda high forecast to build west over the\nsouthwest Atlantic through the forecast period. Numerous to \nwidespread showers (~70-90% chance) and lightning storms are \nforecast to develop each afternoon/evening as collisions occur \nbetween sea breezes, as well as outflow from previous storms. Rain\nshower and storm chances reduce to 50-80% on Sunday and 50-70% \nMonday. Near to above normal high and low temperatures in the \nupper 80s to low 90s and low to upper 70s are forecast.",
